@Joystick Configuration Editor
This menu allows editing the joystick mapping for a world file.

Joystick mapping has sixteen levels numbered from 0 to 15; each level is one
column. Which level is active is set by the program memory; see event.doc for
details about this. Some mappings may also temporarily set the level. The
first (unnumbered) column is the name of the button to map.

There are two pages of mappings, which both work in the same way; push TAB
to switch between the two pages.

The rows are in the order of first precedence to last precedence, in case
there are conflicts. Push SHIFT and arrows to change their order.

If the cursor is at the left side of the screen then push SPACE to set the
button names, with semicolons in between; the first such button name has
precedence. You can push ^P to enter special characters such as arrows in
the button names; you should probably map the names with arrows to the
corresponding arrow keys, since this corresponds to the D-pad or to the
analog axes of the joystick (depending on the user configuration).

When the cursor is in any cell you can set the command:

þ N = Set a normal ASCII mapping; push the key that you want it to be mapped
to which can be any printable ASCII character, or an arrow key, or an arrow
key with SHIFT, or RETURN, or TAB, or BACKSPACE.

þ C = Set a special command code. It will display the list of command codes
and you can select one. Some commands are only valid in some contexts.

þ 1 to 8 = Temporarily shift to a different level while the button is being
held down. In this way, you can implement mappings to combinations of buttons
such as holding the A button and the D-pad to shoot.

þ X = Clear the mapping, so that the button has no effect.

þ A = Toggle auto-fire. This is only valid for normal mappings. If auto-fire
is enabled then the function of that button continues while it is held.

